<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Astrakhan</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xacitarxan, Hajji Tarkhan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Astrakhan is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> delta near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. Its predecessor, Hajji Tarkhan or Xacitarxan, was a trading town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and from the 15th century the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV of Russia</a> conquered the khanate in 1556, and the city, rebuilt on a nearby site with a stone kremlin, became Russia's gateway to the Caspian.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/astrakhan.jpg" alt="Astrakhan" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ic.pics.livejournal.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Astrakhan_-_Russia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Astrakhan lies on two banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, in the upper part of the Volga Delta, on eleven islands of the Caspian Depression, about 100 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. At 28 metres below sea level it is the lowest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The delta is rich in sturgeon. The name began as the Russian form of Hajji Tarkhan, from hajji, the title of a Muslim who has made the pilgrimage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, and tarkhan, a term in the Mongol states for a man not subject to taxation. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> legend, first recorded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>, says the settlement was founded by a Turkic pilgrim whose piety earned the future city exemption from taxation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Travellers first mention the town in the early 13th century as Xacitarxan. Timur burned it to the ground in 1395 in his war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and from 1459 to 1556 it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a> of the Astrakhan Tatars. Ivan the Terrible conquered the khanate in 1556 and had a kremlin built on a steep hill overlooking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> in 1558, traditionally taken as the foundation of the modern city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> besieged it in 1569 and retreated, and a year later the sultan renounced his claims, opening the whole Volga to Russian traffic. In the 17th century merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, Safavid Persia, Mughal India and the Khivan Khanate settled there.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepan-razin">Stenka Razin</a> and his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> held the city for seventeen months in 1670 and 1671, and in 1717 it became the seat of the Astrakhan Governorate. Fires struck it in 1702, 1718 and 1767, and cholera killed much of the population in 1830. In March 1919, after a failed workers' revolt against Bolshevik rule, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cheka">Cheka</a> executed between 3,000 and 5,000 people in less than a week. It became the seat of a Soviet oblast in 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Astrakhan is the largest city and administrative centre of Astrakhan Oblast, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with 475,629 inhabitants at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. For some 400 years it has been Russia's main centre of fish processing. The Kremlin has two cathedrals, consecrated in 1700 and 1710, built by masters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a>, which keep traditional Russian features while their exterior decoration is baroque. The city has a large Muslim population, including Astrakhan Tatars, and the White Mosque dates from 1777.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Astrakhan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its successor states, the khanates of Kazan, Crimea, <b>Astrakhan</b> and Sibir, carried its political traditions into the early modern period, the Crimean Khanate surviving until 1783, and the Volga and Crimean Tatars, the Kazakhs and the Uzbeks all trace parts of their history to the Ulus of Jochi.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>His reign brought the conquest of Kazan and <b>Astrakhan</b> as well as the terror of the oprichnina.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-16">16 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Astrakhan Khanate formed in the 1460s around the town of <b>Xacitarxan</b> near the Volga delta, under khans descended from Küchük Muhammad of the Great Horde.</blockquote><a href="/state/astrakhan-khanate">Astrakhan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Its remnants retreated towards <b>Astrakhan</b>, and Soviet rule returned to the North Caucasus only in 1920.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-caucasian-soviet-republic">North Caucasian Soviet Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>The host traditionally dates its founding to 1577, when Russian Cossacks were recorded on the Terek after Muscovy's push down the Volga to <b>Astrakhan</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/terek-cossack-host">Terek Cossack Host</a></li></ul></section>
